ed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the four stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** | **D** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $a$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $0$ | $c$ | $1$ |  |\n| **D** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are $2$ arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station D. So, there are two routes from $A$ to $D,$ and so $a=2.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $C,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station C. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $C,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** | **D** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n| **D** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-139719/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139689/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "ba39b93f-7e33-570a-ba81-c570bc0f4f57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139689/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f32746fa-b21d-5419-89a6-e1ebe75e57bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139689/selectio